---
import AppLayout from '../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import TradingSimulator from '../components/trading/TradingSimulator.tsx';
---

<AppLayout
  title="Paper Trading Simulator | Stockade"
  description="Practice paper trading with our free stock market simulator. Candlestick charts, EMA indicators & $100K virtual balance. No signup required."
>
  <div class="flex flex-col h-full">
    <h1 id="simulator-h1" class="sr-only">Paper Trading Simulator</h1>
    <p
      class="shrink-0 px-4 py-2 border-b border-[var(--c-border)] bg-[var(--c-bg-soft)] text-[12px] leading-relaxed text-[var(--c-text-muted)]"
    >
      Trade 29 simulated stocks, crypto pairs, forex and futures with a
      <span class="font-mono text-[var(--c-text)]">$100,000</span> virtual balance — market and limit orders,
      stop-loss and take-profit, live candlesticks. No signup, no real money.
      <a href="#how-to-use" class="text-[#f59e0b] hover:underline">How it works ↓</a>
    </p>
    <div class="flex-1 min-h-0">
      <TradingSimulator client:load />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section
    slot="below"
    id="how-to-use"
    class="border-t border-[var(--c-border)] bg-[var(--c-bg-soft)]"
  >
    <div class="max-w-[820px] mx-auto px-6 py-12">
      <details class="group" open>
        <summary
          class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
        >
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--c-text)]">
            How to Use This Simulator
          </h2>
          <svg
            class="shrink-0 text-[var(--c-text-subtle)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
            width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"
          >
            <path d="M3 6l5 5 5-5" />
          </svg>
        </summary>

        <div class="mt-6">
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            Stockade's paper trading simulator gives you a $100,000 virtual account, a live-ticking
            candlestick chart, and a full order ticket — no signup and no real money at risk. Your
            balance and trade history are saved in your browser, so you can pick up where you left off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            Reading the interface
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The chart fills the centre of the screen. The top bar holds the symbol selector — 29
            simulated tickers grouped into stocks, crypto, forex and futures — along with timeframe
            buttons and toggles for the EMA 9, EMA 20 and EMA 50 overlays. A volume histogram runs
            along the bottom of the chart. The right-hand panel is your account: current balance, any
            open position with its unrealised P&amp;L, and a running log of every fill. On narrow
            screens those panels collapse into the Chart, Order and Trades tabs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            Placing an order
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            Enter a quantity, then choose an order type. A market order fills immediately at the last
            traded price. A limit order rests until the market reaches your price — a buy placed
            below the market fills when price falls to it, a sell placed above the market fills when
            price rises to it — and resting orders appear under Working Orders, where you can cancel
            them. A limit placed on the wrong side of the market is already marketable, so it fills
            straight away at the better prevailing price. The optional stop-loss and take-profit
            fields work as an OCO bracket — whichever level price reaches first closes the position
            and clears the other. <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">Buy</strong> opens
            a long, <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">Sell</strong> opens a short,
            and <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">Flatten</strong> closes everything
            at the current price. Once the page has focus you can trade from the keyboard:
            <kbd class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[#f59e0b]">B</kbd> to buy,
            <kbd class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[#f59e0b]">S</kbd> to sell,
            <kbd class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[#f59e0b]">F</kbd> to flatten.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            What this simulator does not model
          </h3>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Fills here are simpler than a live exchange, and it is worth knowing exactly how before
            you read too much into a result:
          </p>
          <ul class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">No slippage.</strong> Every order
              fills at the exact price shown, with no spread and no commission. In a real market a
              fast move or a thin book routinely fills you worse than the price you clicked.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">No partial fills.</strong> Orders
              fill in full and instantly, whatever the size. A real venue can fill a large order in
              pieces at several prices, or leave part of it unfilled.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">No queue position.</strong> A
              resting limit order fills the moment price reaches its level, as if it were first in
              line. On a real book you sit behind everyone who posted at that price ahead of you, and
              price can touch your level without filling you at all.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">Resting orders don't reserve
              buying power.</strong> Balance is only checked when a resting order actually fills, so
              an order can be cancelled at that moment for insufficient funds rather than being
              blocked when you place it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-[var(--c-text)] font-semibold">Prices are generated, not real.</strong> All
              29 tickers are fictional and their price movement is synthetic. Nothing here is
              live market data, and no symbol represents a real security.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed mt-3">
            The upshot: treat the results as practice for process — entries, exits, sizing, and
            discipline — rather than as a forecast of what the same strategy would earn with real
            money.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">
            Tips for getting started
          </h3>
          <ul class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li>Risk a fixed 1–2% of your account per trade rather than sizing by gut feel.</li>
            <li>Set a stop loss before you enter, not after the trade has moved against you.</li>
            <li>
              Switch to replay mode to step through candles at your own pace — the spacebar advances one
              bar.
            </li>
            <li>
              Stick with one symbol for a stretch; repeated exposure teaches more than jumping between
              all 29.
            </li>
            <li>
              Review your results on the <a href="/analytics" class="text-[#f59e0b] hover:underline">Analytics
              dashboard</a> — win rate and profit factor say more than one lucky trade.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppLayout>

<script>
  const symbol = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('symbol');
  if (symbol) {
    const h1 = document.getElementById('simulator-h1');
    if (h1) h1.textContent = `Trade ${symbol.toUpperCase()} — Paper Trading Simulator`;
  }
</script>
